Keep the Windows host tools on the build machine's own architecture

Node's snapshot tools - mksnapshot, torque, and the builtins they generate - are built
and then run by the same build, so they belong to gyp's "host" toolset. The ninja
generator picked the toolchain environment from the architecture in the configuration
(msvs_configuration_platform), which names the *target*, so the host toolset inherited
the target's toolchain too. A native build never notices, because there the two are the
same machine. Cross-building Windows arm64 from an x64 machine compiled mksnapshot with
the arm64 toolchain, and the step after it could not start the .exe it had just
produced.

The host toolset now asks what architecture this machine is and uses that. A native
build lands on the value it already had - an x64 machine building x64 changes nothing -
and `--cross-compiling` is deliberately left alone: Node needs it to get the toolset
split at all, and the split is now spelled correctly in the generated files.

Checked without a Windows machine by generating the ninja files with the Windows
flavour for an arm64 target (bash tools/local_windows_ninja_check.sh --arch arm64):

    obj/tools/v8_gypfiles/v8_snapshot.host.ninja   arch = environment.x64
    obj/node_base.ninja                            arch = environment.arm64

  before this patch the host file said environment.arm64 and named $cl_arm64.

--- a/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/generator/ninja.py
+++ b/tools/gyp/pylib/gyp/generator/ninja.py
@@ -24,6 +24,14 @@
 from gyp import MSVSUtil, ninja_syntax
 from gyp.common import GetEnvironFallback
 
+
+def _host_machine():
+    """GodotJS: architecture of the machine generating these files, lower case."""
+    import platform
+
+    return platform.machine().lower()
+
+
 generator_default_variables = {
     "EXECUTABLE_PREFIX": "",
     "EXECUTABLE_SUFFIX": "",
@@ -402,6 +410,20 @@
         if self.flavor == "win":
             self.msvs_settings = gyp.msvs_emulation.MsvsSettings(spec, generator_flags)
             arch = self.msvs_settings.GetArch(config_name)
+            if spec["toolset"] == "host":
+                # GodotJS: a host-toolset object runs on the machine doing the build, so
+                # it has to be compiled with that machine's toolchain. Cross-building
+                # Windows arm64 from an x64 runner names the arm64 environment here, and
+                # the mksnapshot.exe it produces cannot be started by that runner.
+                host_arch = {
+                    "x86_64": "x64",
+                    "amd64": "x64",
+                    "aarch64": "arm64",
+                    "arm64": "arm64",
+                    "i686": "x86",
+                    "x86": "x86",
+                }.get(_host_machine(), arch)
+                arch = host_arch if host_arch in self.win_env else arch
             self.ninja.variable("arch", self.win_env[arch])
             self.ninja.variable("cc", "$cl_" + arch)
             self.ninja.variable("cxx", "$cl_" + arch)
